                <pb facs="0991" n="921" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg921"/>

                <head>No. 594.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Baker to Mr. Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Caracas</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-05-15"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 15, 1883</date>.
                        (Received June 6.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 688.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my No. 686 of date 11th
                    instant relative to the matter of the custody of ships’ papers, I have to say
                    that Colonel Mansfield, the British minister at this capital, has since
                    indicated to me in conversation that he entirely approved my memorandum in the
                    premises, and that he had addressed a note to Mr. Seijas fully concurring
                    therein; whilst Mr. Pyer, the German minister here, has since indicated to me in
                    conversation, that having no instruction from his Government on the point, he
                    could take no action thereon, but would report the matter of my memorandum to
                    his Government.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JEHU BAKER.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
